    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d168">

                <pb facs="0636" n="595" sameAs="frus:frus1981-88v04/pg595"/>

                <head>168. Telegram From the Embassy in the Soviet Union to the Department of
                        State<anchor n="1" xml:id="d168-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Moscow</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1984-02-07T12:01:00+00:00">February 7, 1984,
                        1201Z</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1487. For Under Secretary Eagleburger Only From Hartman. Subject: Secretary’s
                    Meeting With Dobrynin.</p>
                <p>1. Confidential—Entire text.</p>
                <pb facs="0637" n="596" xml:id="pg596"/>
                <p>2. Just back last weekend, and am naturally anxious for a read-out on the
                    Secretary’s last meeting with Dobrynin,<anchor n="2" xml:id="d168-f2"/> part of
                    which I understand was one-on-one. I would also appreciate the text of the
                    high-level message that Dobrynin gave the Secretary.<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d168-f3"/></p>
                <p>3. When the Secretary returns, it will be important to pick up on some of the
                    things which were discussed at Stockholm—in particular, the strategic arms
                    framework, the consulates agreement, and the exchanges agreement. I will send
                    some thoughts on the exchanges agreement in a few days.</p>
                <p>4. We all had a feeling there was a little bit of movement and we should be sure
                    it doesn’t stop for lack of imagination on our side. Important point to keep
                    remembering these days is that any visible movement in the relation toward more
                    constructive directions is in our interest and helps to knock down Soviet public
                    campaign that things have never been worse. Therefore, we should let loose a few
                    initiatives.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed><hi rendition="#bold">Hartman</hi></signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
